# John Henry: traditional African-American ballad, printed from 1909.
{title: John Henry}
{composer: Traditional (USA)}
{key: G}
{tempo: 104}
{time: 4/4}
{x_level: 2}
{x_strum: D - D U - U D U}

{start_of_grid label="Verse"}
| G . . . | G . . . |
| G . . . | G . . . |
| C . . . | C . . . |
| G . . . | G . . . |
| G . . . | D7 . . . | G . . . | G . . . |
{end_of_grid}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
When [G]John Henry was a little baby,
Sitting on his daddy's knee,
He [C]picked up a hammer and a piece of steel,
Said, "[G]Hammer's gonna be the death of me,
Lord, Lord, [D7]hammer's gonna be the [G]death of me."
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
The [G]captain said to John Henry,
"Gonna bring that steam drill 'round,
Gonna [C]bring that steam drill out on the job,
Gonna [G]whop that steel on down,
Lord, Lord, [D7]gonna whop that [G]steel on down."
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
John [G]Henry said to his captain,
"A man ain't nothin' but a man,
But be[C]fore I let your steam drill beat me down,
I'll [G]die with a hammer in my hand,
Lord, Lord, I'll [D7]die with a [G]hammer in my hand."
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 4"}
John [G]Henry hammered in the mountain,
His hammer was striking fire,
He [C]hammered so hard, he broke his poor heart,
And he [G]laid down his hammer and he died,
Lord, Lord, he [D7]laid down his hammer and [G]died.
{end_of_verse}

{comment: A shortened ballad: four of its many traditional verses.}
{x_end: G}
